<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djoumbe Fatima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1836/37–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Fatima bint Abderremane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djoumbe Fatima was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Mohéli</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, succeeding her father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, a Merina prince, as a child in 1842. Her reign was marked by rivalry between French and British interests, and she abdicated in favour of her son in 1867, returning to power for a time later. Her dealings with foreign powers led towards French control of the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djoumbe-fatima.jpg" alt="Djoumbe Fatima" width="320" height="562" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Trinquart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djoumbe_Fatima_1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Djoumbé Fatima, also known as Djoumbé Soudi or Queen Jumbe-Souli, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, also known as Sultan Abderahmane, a Malagasy prince who had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> after the death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a>, whose brother-in-law he was. Her mother, Ravao, daughter of Andriantsiahofa of Imamo West, was Merina. Fatima was a cousin of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-ii">Radama II</a> of Madagascar and had a sister, Jumbe-Salama, who died young.</p><p>When her father died in 1842, Ravao ruled for a time as regent and in 1843 married his former adviser, General Ratsivandini. The couple divorced in 1846, and Ravao died by poison in 1847. Ratsivandini became the young queen's tutor and began to arrange her marriage to the sultan of Zanzibar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had recently acquired Mayotte and wished to extend its presence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Mohéli</a>. The French sent a governess, Madame Droit, to the queen and arranged her coronation in 1849, at the age of 12. Two years later Fatima dismissed the governess and married Saïd Mohammed Nasser M'Kadar, a cousin of the sultan of Zanzibar, who became prince consort and ruled beside her until the French ousted him in 1860. Like most of the island's people she was Muslim, and she did not convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, although the missionary David Griffiths worked on Mohéli.</p><p>In 1863 a French delegation visited her, recorded by the photographer Désiré Charnay, who found her melancholy and sickly, dressed in a robe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and gold with her face covered. The delegation was sent to persuade her of the advantages of becoming a French colony, and she resisted. Her palace overlooked the sea beside a garrison of 28 soldiers. She later married twice more and gave up the throne for her son, before being restored by the French and ruling until her death in 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Three sons were born of her marriage to M'Kadar, two of whom ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Mohéli</a>: Mohamed bin Saidi Hamadi Makadara and Abderremane bin Saidi Hamadi Makadara, who reigned from 1878 to 1885. From her relationship with Emile Fleuriot de Langle, an assistant to the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-lambert">Joseph-François Lambert</a>, came two more children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salima-machamba">Salima Machamba</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fomboni">Fomboni</a> in 1874, who became sultana of Mohéli in 1888, married a French gendarme, Camille Paule, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Djoumbé Fatima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Ramanetaka's death in 1842 his young daughter <b>Djoumbe Fatima</b> became queen.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-moheli">Sultanate of Moheli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ouallah. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fomboni">Fomboni</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.33,43.67&amp;year=1233" aria-label="Ouallah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Djoumbe Fatima"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6131 6047h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6131 6047h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.33,43.67&amp;year=1233">What was here in 1233? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
